orthant 1 language

- # `↯/s.105.4` — `Asn1.Error`
+ # `↯/s.105.4` — `Asn1.Error`

- Raised when Enigmatic cannot read a DER-encoded ASN.1 value.
+ Levée lorsque Enigmatic ne parvient pas à lire une valeur ASN.1 encodée en DER.

- ## Cause
+ ## Cause

- DER (the Distinguished Encoding Rules) is the canonical subset of BER:
- every value has exactly one valid encoding. Enigmatic's decoder is
- strict, so anything BER permits but DER does not — indefinite lengths,
- overlong lengths and tag numbers, non-minimal integers, constructed
- strings, unordered sets — is an error rather than something to be
- tolerated. That strictness is what lets a value that was read back be
- re-encoded to the bytes it came from, which is what verifying a
- signature over a certificate's `TBSCertificate` depends on. The message
- is `could not process the ASN.1 value because $reason`, and every reason
- carries the byte offset at which the problem was found.
+ DER (les Distinguished Encoding Rules) est le sous-ensemble canonique de BER :
+ chaque valeur a exactement un encodage valide. Le décodeur d'Enigmatic est
+ strict, donc tout ce que BER permet mais que DER interdit — longueurs indéfinies,
+ longueurs et numéros de tag trop longs, entiers non minimaux, chaînes construites,
+ ensembles non triés — est une erreur plutôt que quelque chose à tolérer. C'est
+ cette rigueur qui permet de réencoder une valeur relue vers les octets dont elle
+ provient, ce dont dépend la vérification d'une signature portant sur le
+ `TBSCertificate` d'un certificat. Le message est
+ `could not process the ASN.1 value because $reason`, et chaque raison porte la
+ position, en octets, à laquelle le problème a été trouvé.

- ## Variants
+ ## Variantes

- ```tabular
+ ```tabular

- column  Code
+ column  Code

- column  Reason
- column  Meaning
+ column  Raison
+ column  Signification

- column  Message
+ column  Message

- column  Resolution
- row  `↯/s.105.4.1`  `Truncated`  The input ended before the current value did.  `[↯/s.105.4.1] could not process the ASN.1 value because the input was truncated at byte 42`  Confirm the input is complete; a PEM body that was cut short, or a length prefix read from the wrong offset, are the usual causes.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.2`  `IndefiniteLength`  A value used BER's indefinite-length form, which DER forbids.  `[↯/s.105.4.2] could not process the ASN.1 value because an indefinite length was found at byte 1`  Re-encode the input as DER; `openssl asn1parse` will show where the indefinite lengths are.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.3`  `NonMinimalLength`  A length was not written in the fewest possible bytes: either the long form was used for a length below 128, or its leading byte was zero.  `[↯/s.105.4.3] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong length was found at byte 1`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.4`  `NonMinimalTag`  A tag number below 31 was written in the high-tag-number form, or a high tag number had a leading zero group.  `[↯/s.105.4.4] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong tag number was found at byte 1`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.5`  `NonMinimalInteger`  An integer carried a redundant leading `0x00` or `0xFF` byte. DER requires the shortest two's-complement representation.  `[↯/s.105.4.5] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong integer was found at byte 0`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.6`  `EmptyInteger`  An integer had no content octets; ASN.1 requires at least one.  `[↯/s.105.4.6] could not process the ASN.1 value because an integer with no content was found at byte 0`  Check the producer of the input: a zero must be encoded as the single byte `0x00`.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.7`  `Overflow`  A length or tag number was too large to represent: a length needing more than four bytes, a length above `Int.MaxValue`, or a tag number above `Int.MaxValue`.  `[↯/s.105.4.7] could not process the ASN.1 value because an unrepresentable length was found at byte 1`  Values this large are almost always a sign that the input is not DER at all, or that parsing began at the wrong offset.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.8`  `Trailing`  A complete value was read, but bytes remained after it. A DER document holds exactly one value.  `[↯/s.105.4.8] could not process the ASN.1 value because unexpected trailing bytes were found from byte 2`  If the input holds several concatenated values — a certificate chain, for instance — split it before decoding.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.9`  `InvalidUtf8`  A string's content octets were not valid UTF-8.  `[↯/s.105.4.9] could not process the ASN.1 value because invalid UTF-8 was found at byte 12`  Check the encoding of the source text; ASN.1 string types other than `UTF8String` may carry a different character set.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.10`  `ReservedTag`  Universal tag number 0 was found; it is reserved by X.690.  `[↯/s.105.4.10] could not process the ASN.1 value because the reserved tag number 0 was found at byte 0`  The input is not valid ASN.1; check that decoding began at the start of a value.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.11`  `BadOid`  An object identifier was malformed: it had no content, or its final subidentifier's continuation bit was set, or a subidentifier began with the padding byte `0x80`.  `[↯/s.105.4.11] could not process the ASN.1 value because a malformed object identifier was found at byte 3`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.12`  `OidArcOverflow`  An object identifier arc was too large for an `Int`. Arcs this large occur under the UUID arc `2.25`, which Enigmatic's `Asn1.ObjectId` does not yet model.  `[↯/s.105.4.12] could not process the ASN.1 value because an arc too large for Int was found at byte 3`  Read the value as raw bytes for now; wider arcs are a planned extension.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.13`  `UnsortedSet`  The members of a `SET` were not in ascending order of their encodings, as X.690 §11.6 requires.  `[↯/s.105.4.13] could not process the ASN.1 value because the set at byte 5 was not in ascending order`  Re-encode the input as DER; encoders that emit BER often leave `SET` members in their declaration order.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.14`  `BadTime`  A `UTCTime` or `GeneralizedTime` was not in DER's single permitted form — `YYMMDDHHMMSSZ` and `YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ` respectively — or named a date that does not exist.  `[↯/s.105.4.14] could not process the ASN.1 value because a malformed time value was found at byte 0`  Re-encode the input as DER: fractional seconds, offsets from UTC and omitted seconds are all forbidden.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.15`  `BadBoolean`  A boolean's content octet was neither `0x00` nor `0xFF`. BER allows any non-zero byte to mean `true`; DER does not.  `[↯/s.105.4.15] could not process the ASN.1 value because a boolean with the content byte 1 was found at byte 0`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.16`  `BadLength`  A value's length was not one its type permits: a `NULL` with content, a boolean whose length was not 1, or a `BIT STRING` with no content.  `[↯/s.105.4.16] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 5 had the invalid length 1 at byte 0`  Check the producer of the input.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.17`  `BadUnusedBits`  A `BIT STRING`'s leading octet declared more than seven unused bits, declared unused bits for an empty string, or the bits it declared unused were not zero.  `[↯/s.105.4.17] could not process the ASN.1 value because a bit string declaring 8 unused bits was found at byte 0`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.18`  `NotPrimitive`  A type that DER requires to be primitive — the string types, integers, `BIT STRING`, `OCTET STRING`, `NULL`, object identifiers and the two time types — was encoded in constructed form.  `[↯/s.105.4.18] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 4 was encoded in constructed form at byte 0`  Re-encode the input as DER; segmented (constructed) strings are a BER feature.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.19`  `NotConstructed`  A `SEQUENCE` or `SET` was encoded in primitive form.  `[↯/s.105.4.19] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 16 was encoded in primitive form at byte 0`  The input is not valid ASN.1; check that decoding began at the start of a value.
+ column  Résolution
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.1`  `Truncated`  L'entrée s'est terminée avant la valeur en cours.  `[↯/s.105.4.1] could not process the ASN.1 value because the input was truncated at byte 42`  Vérifiez que l'entrée est complète ; un corps PEM tronqué, ou un préfixe de longueur lu à la mauvaise position, en sont les causes habituelles.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.2`  `IndefiniteLength`  Une valeur a utilisé la forme à longueur indéfinie de BER, que DER interdit.  `[↯/s.105.4.2] could not process the ASN.1 value because an indefinite length was found at byte 1`  Réencodez l'entrée en DER ; `openssl asn1parse` montre où se trouvent les longueurs indéfinies.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.3`  `NonMinimalLength`  Une longueur n'a pas été écrite sur le moins d'octets possible : soit la forme longue a été utilisée pour une longueur inférieure à 128, soit son premier octet était nul.  `[↯/s.105.4.3] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong length was found at byte 1`  Réencodez l'entrée en DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.4`  `NonMinimalTag`  Un numéro de tag inférieur à 31 a été écrit sous la forme des numéros de tag élevés, ou un numéro de tag élevé avait un groupe initial nul.  `[↯/s.105.4.4] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong tag number was found at byte 1`  Réencodez l'entrée en DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.5`  `NonMinimalInteger`  Un entier portait un octet initial `0x00` ou `0xFF` redondant. DER exige la représentation en complément à deux la plus courte.  `[↯/s.105.4.5] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong integer was found at byte 0`  Réencodez l'entrée en DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.6`  `EmptyInteger`  Un entier n'avait aucun octet de contenu ; ASN.1 en exige au moins un.  `[↯/s.105.4.6] could not process the ASN.1 value because an integer with no content was found at byte 0`  Vérifiez ce qui produit l'entrée : un zéro doit être encodé par l'unique octet `0x00`.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.7`  `Overflow`  Une longueur ou un numéro de tag était trop grand pour être représenté : une longueur nécessitant plus de quatre octets, une longueur supérieure à `Int.MaxValue`, ou un numéro de tag supérieur à `Int.MaxValue`.  `[↯/s.105.4.7] could not process the ASN.1 value because an unrepresentable length was found at byte 1`  Des valeurs aussi grandes indiquent presque toujours que l'entrée n'est pas du tout du DER, ou que l'analyse a commencé à la mauvaise position.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.8`  `Trailing`  Une valeur complète a été lue, mais des octets restaient après elle. Un document DER contient exactement une valeur.  `[↯/s.105.4.8] could not process the ASN.1 value because unexpected trailing bytes were found from byte 2`  Si l'entrée contient plusieurs valeurs concaténées — une chaîne de certificats, par exemple —, découpez-la avant de la décoder.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.9`  `InvalidUtf8`  Les octets de contenu d'une chaîne n'étaient pas de l'UTF-8 valide.  `[↯/s.105.4.9] could not process the ASN.1 value because invalid UTF-8 was found at byte 12`  Vérifiez l'encodage du texte source ; les types de chaînes ASN.1 autres que `UTF8String` peuvent porter un autre jeu de caractères.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.10`  `ReservedTag`  Le numéro de tag universel 0 a été trouvé ; il est réservé par X.690.  `[↯/s.105.4.10] could not process the ASN.1 value because the reserved tag number 0 was found at byte 0`  L'entrée n'est pas de l'ASN.1 valide ; vérifiez que le décodage a commencé au début d'une valeur.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.11`  `BadOid`  Un identifiant d'objet était malformé : il n'avait aucun contenu, ou le bit de continuation de son dernier sous-identifiant était positionné, ou un sous-identifiant commençait par l'octet de remplissage `0x80`.  `[↯/s.105.4.11] could not process the ASN.1 value because a malformed object identifier was found at byte 3`  Réencodez l'entrée en DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.12`  `OidArcOverflow`  Un arc d'identifiant d'objet était trop grand pour un `Int`. Des arcs aussi grands apparaissent sous l'arc UUID `2.25`, que l'`Asn1.ObjectId` d'Enigmatic ne modélise pas encore.  `[↯/s.105.4.12] could not process the ASN.1 value because an arc too large for Int was found at byte 3`  Pour l'instant, lisez la valeur sous forme d'octets bruts ; des arcs plus larges sont une extension prévue.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.13`  `UnsortedSet`  Les membres d'un `SET` n'étaient pas dans l'ordre croissant de leurs encodages, comme l'exige X.690 §11.6.  `[↯/s.105.4.13] could not process the ASN.1 value because the set at byte 5 was not in ascending order`  Réencodez l'entrée en DER ; les encodeurs qui émettent du BER laissent souvent les membres d'un `SET` dans leur ordre de déclaration.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.14`  `BadTime`  Un `UTCTime` ou un `GeneralizedTime` n'était pas dans l'unique forme que permet DER — respectivement `YYMMDDHHMMSSZ` et `YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ` — ou désignait une date qui n'existe pas.  `[↯/s.105.4.14] could not process the ASN.1 value because a malformed time value was found at byte 0`  Réencodez l'entrée en DER : les fractions de seconde, les décalages par rapport à UTC et l'omission des secondes sont tous interdits.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.15`  `BadBoolean`  L'octet de contenu d'un booléen n'était ni `0x00` ni `0xFF`. BER permet à tout octet non nul de signifier `true` ; DER non.  `[↯/s.105.4.15] could not process the ASN.1 value because a boolean with the content byte 1 was found at byte 0`  Réencodez l'entrée en DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.16`  `BadLength`  La longueur d'une valeur n'était pas une longueur que permet son type : un `NULL` avec du contenu, un booléen dont la longueur n'était pas 1, ou un `BIT STRING` sans contenu.  `[↯/s.105.4.16] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 5 had the invalid length 1 at byte 0`  Vérifiez ce qui produit l'entrée.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.17`  `BadUnusedBits`  L'octet initial d'un `BIT STRING` déclarait plus de sept bits inutilisés, déclarait des bits inutilisés pour une chaîne vide, ou les bits qu'il déclarait inutilisés n'étaient pas nuls.  `[↯/s.105.4.17] could not process the ASN.1 value because a bit string declaring 8 unused bits was found at byte 0`  Réencodez l'entrée en DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.18`  `NotPrimitive`  Un type que DER exige primitif — les types de chaînes, les entiers, `BIT STRING`, `OCTET STRING`, `NULL`, les identifiants d'objets et les deux types temporels — a été encodé sous forme construite.  `[↯/s.105.4.18] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 4 was encoded in constructed form at byte 0`  Réencodez l'entrée en DER ; les chaînes segmentées (construites) sont une fonctionnalité de BER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.19`  `NotConstructed`  Un `SEQUENCE` ou un `SET` a été encodé sous forme primitive.  `[↯/s.105.4.19] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 16 was encoded in primitive form at byte 0`  L'entrée n'est pas de l'ASN.1 valide ; vérifiez que le décodage a commencé au début d'une valeur.

